---
description: Documentation writing style
alwaysApply: false
---
When writing documentation:
- Explan everything with scientific tone but only very simple language
- Do not use complicated long words
- Short sentences
- Style of writting a book 
- Explain what it do why and where it is used
- Then write technicalities
- Allways add as many numbers/references as posisble 
- Add a lot of function names and so on
- Write text as short as possible
- NEVER USE ** 
- Every description of action should have: Generating actions,  Executing actions, Comparison with the original growingNN paper, Known limitations
- Documentation is in obsydian only so if possible add to what other apge some page relates to create good dependency graph, for example: "It is used by [[Model Analyser]] and [[Model Transformer]] when they ..."
- Prefer many short .md files than one long .md file 
- Create new files and folders 
- There should not be anything like index, connections should see what relates to what index and list of all things is destroying it
- No index page
- Do not add a `### Related` section. Put cross-links only inside the body text where they fit naturally.
- Pages under `documentation/obsydian/growingNN/tests/` are outside the vault link graph: never use Obsidian wiki links `[[...]]` or embed tags `![[...]]` there; describe modules and code with plain names and repo paths.
- Nothing outside `documentation/obsydian/growingNN/tests/` may mention the repo `tests/` folder, test scripts, test drivers, or any page under `documentation/obsydian/growingNN/tests/`. Other vault pages must not wiki-link to test pages; cite product code paths instead.
- Tests should not be linked by tag to any onter page
- Any page should not link to test describeing pages 
